				<h1>Clannad, the Enya's family band</h1>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" target="_blank" href="https://amzn.to/3SU09Xt" >
    <Image  src={vbe} alt="All Enya songs: Complete list for listening and download"  width="300" height="300" /></a>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/gp/redirect.html?ie=UTF8&location=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.amazon.com%2FClannad%2Fartist%2FB000APWCWO%2F&tag=enylyrallenys-20&linkCode=ur2&camp=1789&creative=9325"><strong>Clannad</strong></a> is a Grammy Award-winning Irish musical group.</p>

<p>They were formed by several siblings of <strong><a href="/enya-biography/">Enya</a></strong>.</p>

<p>They have adopted various musical styles throughout their history, including folk, folk rock, traditional Irish, Celtic, and <a href="/new-age-music/">New Age music</a>.</p>

<p>Their sound often incorporates elements of smooth jazz and Gregorian chant.</p>

<p>They are known for performing in various languages, including English, Latin, Gàidhlig, and Mohican.</p>

<p>Most of all, they are celebrated for singing in their native tongue, Irish.</p>

<a href="https://amzn.to/2ZIw9PC/104-2752662-1547131?%5Fencoding=UTF8&amp;node=11" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener" class="new-amazon-album-link"><Image src={img300pxClannad334} alt=""  width="300" height="300" /> 

<p><em>Clannad at the Ireland Music Awards.</em></p>
</a>



<h2>The Origins of Clannad</h2>

<p>Formed in <strong>1970</strong>, the group was created by siblings <strong>Ciarán Brennan</strong>, <strong>Pól Brennan</strong>, and <strong>Moya Brennan</strong> alongside their twin uncles <strong>Noel Duggan</strong> and <strong>Pádraig Duggan</strong>.</p>

<p>The band was initially known as <strong>An Clann as Dobhar</strong>, which means "the Family from Dore".</p>

<p>In <strong>1973</strong>, they shortened their name to <strong>Clannad</strong> after winning the <strong>Letterkenny Folk Festival</strong>, which earned them their first record deal with <strong>Philips Records</strong>.</p>

<p>By <strong>1979</strong>, they had released three albums and completed a successful tour across Europe and the United States.</p>

<p>From <strong>1980 to 1982</strong>, they expanded into a six-piece group with their sister <strong>Enya</strong> on additional keyboards and vocals, before she departed to launch her historic solo career.</p>

<h2>Band Members and Lineup History</h2>

<h3>Founding Members</h3>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Ciarán Brennan</strong>: Bass, guitar, keyboards, mandolin, vocals (1970–2024)</li>
    <li><strong>Pól Brennan</strong>: Flute, guitar, percussion, whistles, vocals (1970–1990, 2011–2024)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Former Members</h3>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Moya Brennan</strong>: Lead vocals, harp (1970–2024; died 2026)</li>
    <li><strong>Noel Duggan</strong>: Guitar, vocals (1970–2022; died 2022)</li>
    <li><strong>Pádraig Duggan</strong>: Guitar, mandola, mandolin, vocals (1970–2016; died 2016)</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/enya-biography/">Enya</a></strong>: Keyboards, percussion, vocals (1980–1982)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Enya's Siblings and Family Background</h2>

<p>The founding members grew up in <strong>Dore</strong>, an area of <strong>Gweedore</strong> in County Donegal, Ireland.</p>

<p>Gweedore is a <strong>Gaeltacht</strong> region where Irish is the primary spoken language.</p>

<p>They were raised in a Roman Catholic family of accomplished musicians.</p>

<p>Their mother, <strong>Máire "Baba" Brennan (née Duggan)</strong>, was a music teacher and local choir leader.</p>

<p>Their father, <strong>Leo Brennan</strong>, was a performer in the <strong>Slieve Foy Band</strong>, a well-known Irish showband.</p>

<p>In <strong>1968</strong>, the family bought and renovated a pub in Meenaleck named <strong>Leo's Tavern</strong>, where the children performed covers of the Beatles, the Beach Boys, and Joni Mitchell.</p>

<p>Tragically, family patriarch <strong>Leo Brennan</strong> passed away on <strong>22 June 2016</strong> at the age of 90.</p>

<p>Their brother, <strong>Leon Brennan</strong>, also passed away in <strong>December 2021</strong> at age 62.</p>

<h2>Moya Brennan: The First Lady of Celtic Music</h2>

<p><strong>Moya Brennan</strong> (born <strong>Máire Philomena Ní Bhraonáin</strong>) was the lead vocalist and harpist of Clannad.</p>

<p>Revered globally as the "First Lady of Celtic Music", she officially adopted the spelling "Moya" by deed poll in <strong>2009</strong>.</p>

<p>She launched her solo career in <strong>1992</strong> with the album <em>Máire</em> and collaborated with dance group Chicane on the 1999 UK top-ten hit <strong>"Saltwater"</strong>.</p>

<p>Moya was nominated for multiple Grammy Awards, won an Emmy Award, and sang on major film soundtracks including <em>Titanic</em> and <em>King Arthur</em>.</p>

<p>In <strong>2000</strong>, she published her candid autobiography, <em>The Other Side of the Rainbow</em>, sharing details of her faith and musical career.</p>

<p>She received an honorary doctorate from Dublin City University in <strong>2022</strong> and was named <strong>Donegal Person of the Year</strong> in <strong>2023</strong>.</p>

<p>After being diagnosed with pulmonary fibrosis in <strong>2016</strong>, Moya Brennan passed away peacefully at her home in Gweedore on <strong>13 April 2026</strong> at the age of 73.</p>

<h2>Clannad's History and Breakthrough</h2>

<p>In <strong>1982</strong>, Clannad achieved international stardom with <strong>"Theme from Harry's Game"</strong>, written for a UK television drama depicting the Troubles.</p>

<p>It remains the only UK top-10 hit single sung entirely in the Irish language.</p>

<p>During the 1980s and 1990s, they pioneered Celtic pop with hit albums like <em>Macalla</em> (1985) and <em>Anam</em> (1990).</p>

<p>Their track <strong>"In a Lifetime"</strong>, a duet with U2's <strong>Bono</strong>, became an enduring international classic.</p>

<p>After releasing <em>Landmarks</em> (1997), the band entered a long hiatus to focus on individual solo projects.</p>

<h2>The Final Chapter, Loss, and Disbandment</h2>

<p>The original five-piece line-up reunited in <strong>2007</strong> for Glasgow's <strong>Celtic Connections Festival</strong> and received a Lifetime Achievement Award at the Meteor Ireland Music Awards.</p>

<p>In <strong>2013</strong>, they released <em>Nádúr</em>, their first new studio album in 15 years, which featured all five original members.</p>

<p>Founding member <strong>Pádraig Duggan</strong> passed away on <strong>9 August 2016</strong>, marking the first loss among the band members.</p>

<p>In <strong>2020</strong>, Clannad released the career-spanning 50th-anniversary collection <em>In a Lifetime</em>, featuring two new tracks: <strong>"A Celtic Dream"</strong> and <strong>"Who Knows Where the Time Goes"</strong>.</p>

<p>Their global <strong>In a Lifetime Farewell Tour</strong> was paused by the COVID-19 pandemic before resuming in <strong>2021</strong>.</p>

<p>On <strong>15 October 2022</strong>, co-founder <strong>Noel Duggan</strong> passed away suddenly at age 73.</p>

<p>Clannad performed an emotional farewell concert at the <strong>3Arena in Dublin</strong> on <strong>18 February 2023</strong>.</p>

<p>They performed a landmark concert at the <strong>Royal Albert Hall</strong> on <strong>30 October 2024</strong>, presenting a 40th-anniversary performance of <em>Legend</em> alongside original cast members from <em>Robin of Sherwood</em>.</p>

<p>The band made a brief, final one-off appearance in <strong>February 2026</strong> to celebrate the 50th anniversary of <em>Hot Press</em> magazine.</p>

