<h2>Awards, Honors, and Global Legacy</h2>

<p>Clannad earned critical acclaim and global awards, including a <strong>Grammy Award</strong>, a <strong>BAFTA</strong>, an <strong>Ivor Novello Award</strong>, and a <strong>Billboard Music Award</strong>.</p>

<p>They achieved eight UK top-10 albums and sold millions of records globally.</p>

<p>In recognition of their cultural contributions, the <strong>Brennan family</strong> was awarded the <strong>Freedom of County Donegal</strong>.</p>

<h2>Discography Highlights</h2>

<h3>Clannad (1973)</h3>

<p>Their debut album, <em>Clannad</em> (also known as <em>The Pretty Maid</em>), showcased contemporary folk arrangements of traditional Gaelic songs alongside jazz influences.</p>

<h3>Clannad 2 (1975)</h3>

<p>Produced by Planxty founder <strong>Dónal Lunny</strong>, this second album deepened their commitment to singing almost entirely in the Irish language.</p>

<h3>Dúlamán (1976)</h3>

<p>Named after a traditional song about seaweed merchants, <em>Dúlamán</em> became a landmark release for Irish folk music and a staple of their live performances.</p>

<h3>Crann Úll (1980)</h3>

<p><em>Crann Úll</em> (Irish for "Apple Tree") featured Máire's harp work and marked Enya's initial backing vocal appearance on "Gathering Mushrooms".</p>

<h3>Fuaim (1982)</h3>

<p>Meaning "Sound", <em>Fuaim</em> introduced electric synthesizer textures and full membership for Enya, who sang lead on "An Túll" and "Buaireadh an Phósta".</p>

<h3>Magical Ring (1983)</h3>

<p>Featuring "Theme from Harry's Game", this multi-platinum album launched Clannad onto the international pop charts.</p>

<h3>Legend (1984)</h3>

<p>Created as the soundtrack for <em>Robin of Sherwood</em>, <em>Legend</em> won a BAFTA Award, making Clannad the first Irish group to earn the honor.</p>

<h3>Macalla (1985)</h3>

<p>Featuring the hit duet "In a Lifetime" with U2's <strong>Bono</strong>, <em>Macalla</em> remains one of Clannad's most acclaimed albums.</p>

<h3>Sirius (1987)</h3>

<p>Recorded in Los Angeles with rock producers Greg Ladanyi and Russ Kunkel, <em>Sirius</em> was a bold crossover experiment that became a major seller.</p>

<h3>Soundtracks & Anthologies (1989–1990)</h3>

<p>The band scored BBC's <em>Atlantic Realm</em> (1989) and the animated film <em>The Angel and the Soldier Boy</em> (1990), alongside releasing the hit compilation <em>Past Present</em>.</p>

<h3>Anam (1990)</h3>

<p>Meaning "Soul", <em>Anam</em> returned to their lush, acoustic-ethereal arrangements and achieved wide commercial success in the United States.</p>

<h3>Banba (1993)</h3>

<p>Earning a Grammy nomination, <em>Banba</em> featured "I Will Find You", written specifically for the soundtrack of <em>The Last of the Mohicans</em>.</p>

<h3>Lore (1996)</h3>

<p><em>Lore</em> explored themes of displacement and Native American history on tracks like "Trail of Tears" and "Croí Cróga".</p>

<h3>Landmarks (1997)</h3>

<p>Inspired by Donegal's landscapes, <em>Landmarks</em> won the <strong>Grammy Award for Best New Age Album</strong> in 1999.</p>

<h3>Nádúr (2013)</h3>

<p>Meaning "Nature", <em>Nádúr</em> was the band's final studio album of new material and the last to feature original member Pádraig Duggan.</p>

<h3>In a Lifetime (2020)</h3>

<p>A career-retrospective 50th-anniversary compilation featuring two new studio tracks, "A Celtic Dream" and "Who Knows Where the Time Goes".</p>

